WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.020
So tell us a little bit about your grandparents.

2
00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:06.740
What their names were on your mom's side and then on your dad's side?

3
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:18.580
We'll start at my mother's mother was Ira Pots who married a David Duff Towner.

4
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:27.120
So to kind of get you an idea, my mother was born in December the sixth, 1903.

5
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:33.240
So that kind of gives you an idea of where the family come from.

6
00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:43.540
And just to give you a little history my grandfather . . . My mother said when she was a little girl my grandfather was the son of a former slave,

7
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:51.320
and they were living somewhere in a little place called Kipling, Louisiana, somewhere around Sugartown, DeRidder [Louisiana].

8
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:57.060
And they migrated this way with horse and wagon.

9
00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:02.980
She was a little girl because she said they had . . . It had taken them three days to get from there to here.

10
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:13.140
She said she could remember sleeping under the stars until they eventually made it here to the Mossville area.

11
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.520
The old Evergreen Road up in that area, what now belongs to Sasol,

12
00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:24.320
was my mother and father's first home that they had, and their home burnt down.

13
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:33.040
From what my mother says she was a little girl, and they moved up here on the Old Spanish Trail.

14
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.180
What kind of work did he get when he got here?

15
00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:39.560
My grandfather was more or less a self-employed person.

16
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.600
He did a lot of things for himself.

17
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.940
And my grandmother was a carpenter, she could do carpentry work.

18
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:56.760
But my grandfather, he would do a lot of timber work, gardening, raise animals,

19
00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:01.220
and he used to take in . . . He actually sold coal.

20
00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:08.580
He would take different types of wood and he would burn it until it got down to a coal.

21
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.680
He would cool it and the coal was used in homes.

22
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.600
He either sold it or gave it to people.

23
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.440
That's what they burned . . . The coal heaters and stuff.

24
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:25.220
My grandmother, now she was a . . . She could build a chicken coop, or a house, or whatever you wanted in an instant.

25
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:30.680
And my grandfather was more or less the person that was the inside.

26
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:41.600
He did the cooking and watching the kids and Grandmother would be doing . . .and from that my mother's had four brothers and all three,

27
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.220
taking after their mother, was professional carpenters.

28
00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:48.600
They would get together; four guys would get together.

29
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.180
They would put up the house.

30
00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.120
One could frame and box in.

31
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.160
One was an electrician and did hard work.

32
00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:01.920
So you know, that's all I can remember so much about my grandmother and grandfather on my mother's side.

33
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.460
My father went to work on an oil platform.

34
00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:07.600
How old?

35
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:10.640
When he was fourteen.

36
00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:22.460
My dad was born in [1908?] and he went to work on this platform at fourteen years old.

37
00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:26.260
My dad say one day at payday he had been working and what they would do,

38
00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:30.300
they would take your name down and you come up to the house to get your money.

39
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:42.820
And the owner happened to see the name Lemelle, and she told the foreman to go bring that kid Lemelle to her.

40
00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:56.960
And when she brought him to the house she discovered that my father's family, Lemelles, was into their family from the wars.

41
00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.370
François Lemelle.

42
00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:10.600
She kept my dad . . . What they called then was the Big House and gave him a responsibility of helping her with her kids and around in the house.

43
00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:17.820
He raised Marie Belle Gardener.

44
00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.420
He raised her from a baby.

45
00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:28.480
When she was grown and got married to a David Garrison, he followed her.

46
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:34.600
He worked in that family probably seventy years.

47
00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:35.080
True story.

48
00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:36.320
Wow.

49
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.740
Then he ended up coming to work for her here in Lake Charles.

50
00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:42.800
Raised her, raised her kids and her grandkids.

51
00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:44.700
So that's what brought him this way, then?

52
00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.460
That's what brought him this way.

53
00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.520
That's what freed him from where he was at.
